From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: REPLACE-ME <you@example>
Date: Sun, 04 Oct 2026 02:34:13 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/nvenc: carry HDR10+ metadata in AV1 temporal units

libaomenc.c already reads AV_FRAME_DATA_DYNAMIC_HDR_PLUS off the frame and
hands it to the encoder as an itu_t_t35 metadata OBU (add_hdr_plus(), with
the six identity bytes per "HDR10+ AV1 Metadata Handling Specification"
v1.0.1 section 2.1). NVENC cannot take that route: its SDK input hook
(obuPayloadArray) does accept AV1_METADATA_TYPE_ITUT_T35, but where the
driver puts the resulting OBU is not controllable, and measured on this
box the blocks land unevenly - some temporal units receive three, and the
units whose only picture OBU carries show_existing_frame=1 receive none.
Meanwhile the output path is a single memcpy of the locked bitstream in
process_output_surface(), so no AV1 structure is visible to FFmpeg at all.

Do it on the output side, where the temporal unit is in hand. Build the
OBU once per submitted frame in nvenc_store_frame_data() and keep it in
NvencFrameData, because nvenc.c frees the SDK payloads immediately after
nvEncEncodePicture(); then walk the returned buffer per temporal unit
(split on OBU_TEMPORAL_DELIMITER) and splice one metadata OBU in front of
the unit's last picture OBU, which is the picture that unit displays -
the position libaom, SVT-AV1 and NVENC's own native blocks all use. A unit
that already carries an itu_t_t35 OBU is left alone; a unit that cannot be
walked, or that holds no picture OBU, is left alone and counted rather
than guessed at. Like libaomenc no option is added: the carrier acts only
when the frame carries the side data, so an encode without it produces the
same bytes it does today.

Verified here on an RTX 5080 and an RTX 4060 laptop, FFmpeg 8.0.git
(8864fd0) configured --toolchain=msvc --enable-nvenc --enable-ffnvcodec
against ffnvcodec 13.1.15.1, with the metadata produced in-graph by
vf_hdr10plus: 38 assertions / 0 failures. 12 of 12 temporal units carry
exactly one 49-byte T35 OBU with identity b5 00 3c 00 01 04; every
picture's decoded average_maxrgb, maxscl and percentile count equal the
values measured on the same frames before the encoder ran, frame by frame,
read back by two separate builds that agree with each other; the five
re-display units keep their frame_to_show_map_idx values and are precisely
the units that had no block before; stripping the inserted OBUs reproduces
the filter-free encode byte for byte in all 12 units and the decoded
pictures of both streams hash identically; MP4 and Matroska keep 12/12. A
copy of the same stream with every unit's block shifted by one is equal in
size and shape (12237 B, 12 units, one block each) yet the value check
reports 12 of 12 frames wrong on it, so the pass above discriminates.

Verified beyond that fixture, with the same ruler over five settings - 12
and 24 frames, 320x240 / 1920x1080 / 3840x2160, presets p1 and p4, and
-zerolatency 1: every unit carries exactly one block and no delivered
block disagrees with its frame (0 of 12, 0 of 24 mismatched), and an
unpatched build decodes every picture of each stream. Note what that
granularity is: units do hold more than one picture OBU (17 over 12 units
at 320x240, 5 of them show_existing_frame-only), so this delivers one
block per temporal unit, which is one per DISPLAYED picture, not one per
coded frame. That is what a HDR10+ block describes; a consumer wanting
metadata for every coded frame would still need DPB bookkeeping this
patch does not do. Real content was measured after this paragraph
was first written: 12 frames of a Dolby Vision Profile 8 clip
through dovi_apply -> hdr10plus -> av1_nvenc give {1:12} with
twelve distinct payloads at the default p4 and under -zerolatency,
and two binaries built separately from the same shipped bytes
produce the identical file (sha256 5782876ae2c6...), so the
placement is a property of this patch, not of one build.

A ten-times-longer window and a much longer GOP were measured
after that paragraph: 120 pictures of a 4K 24 fps HDR10 subject
(not redistributed here) through hdr10plus into av1_nvenc, once
with -g 240, once with -g 3000 and once with -bf 0. Each of the
three streams carries exactly one block in each of its 120
temporal units, and the same window with the producer taken out of
the graph carries 0 blocks in all 120 units, so the blocks are
attributed to this branch. On that footage the payload moves on
almost every picture (117 distinct values over 120), so agreeing
with a scene cut on WHICH frame changed cannot grade placement
there - a series that changes everywhere matches any shift. What
does grade it is rank agreement against an independently measured
per-picture luminance series: aligned +0.770, the same payload
series shifted by one picture +0.747 late and +0.703 early.
A five-times-longer window was measured after that paragraph too:
600 pictures of the same 4K subject at -g 240, on three legs (the
producer's hold default, hold=150, hold=400). Each leg carries
exactly one block in each of its 600 temporal units, no unit is
bare, and an unpatched build decodes 600 pictures whose payloads
join those units one for one. Rank agreement against the
independently measured per-picture luminance series holds at that
length - aligned +0.9813 against +0.9743 and +0.9758 for the same
series shifted by one picture - and it is not a saturated score
there either: displaced by a whole second the same comparison
scores +0.8813.
Verified here, then, up to a 600-picture window with B-frames and
long GOP, and also on real Dolby Vision footage: a 100-picture
whole-clip pass and a 48-picture 3840x2160 window through this
branch, each with exactly one block in every temporal unit and
zero bare units, against an unpatched build that writes none.
What those windows had to be measured against is worth stating,
because it changed a claim rather than confirming it: in the
streams this encoder returns, the presentation time attached to a
frame is a per-packet counter (0, 1, 2 ... monotonic, verified on
two different subjects), so it carries no reordering information
and cannot be used on its own to decide WHICH picture a block
belongs to. The order there was re-derived from the pictures
themselves - per-frame mean luma, one fitted coding-bias offset,
identity residual 0.071 against 0.663 for the best whole-window
shift and a 0.742 frame-to-frame step - and the attribution
results quoted above are stated on that basis. A scene-cut-heavy
subject was also measured: one 12-picture and one 24-picture window
with a detected cut strictly inside, plus a cut-free control window
on the same stream, and the cut changes nothing about placement.
Driver revisions are deliberately not part of the scope here: the
maintainer's ruling on this platform is that revisions that do not
support the current FFmpeg release are not worth a matrix, so this
reports what NVENC 13.1 on this box does. Nothing here touches the
H.264 or HEVC paths. The walk assumes NVENC emits
obu_has_size_field, which every sample on this box did but which
the SDK does not promise.

Reading the frame is first-match on purpose. A frame from a stream that
already carries HDR10+ - an HEVC decoder attaches the authored block as the
same side-data type, and it survives the format conversions in a normal
graph - therefore travels through this patch untouched, which is what makes
an AV1 re-encode of an HDR10+ master possible without computing anything.
Measured on a studio-authored 4K clip: with no producer in the graph, 12 of
12 units carry one block and it is byte-identical to the authored one; with
the producer in the graph the block is the producer's own (49 B against the
master's 64 B), because patch 0002 replaces the entry rather than appending
a second one - were it to append, the authored entry would stay first and
every first-match reader, this one included, would publish the master's
numbers. Both readings are gated together by six assertions over an
elementary-stream fixture that carries 259 authored blocks.

This patch and 0009 must not both be applied: they carry the same
side data into the same file by different routes, and measured with one
fixture on one box, applying both writes 17 blocks for 12 pictures
(per-unit {1:9, 2:1, 3:2}) because this patch deliberately leaves
whatever the driver already placed alone. Apply one of the two: this one
gives every unit exactly one block ({1:12}), while the payload-array
alternative leaves the five re-display units bare
({0:5, 1:4, 2:1, 3:2}). Both counts are in
p5probe/nvenc-h2h-sig-per-packet-20261003.log.

Signed-off-by: REPLACE-ME <REPLACE-ME>

